      title: "Oligarchy and Democracy",
      body: `## Oligarchy

Timocracy declines into **oligarchy** (from *oligoi*, "few," and *archē*, "rule"), "a constitution based on property assessment, in which the rich rule and the poor have no share" (550c). The private treasure-houses filled with gold destroy timocracy. The rulers find new ways of spending money, and bend the laws to allow it, and their wives too. Each, seeing and envying the others, makes the majority like himself. "The more they value money-making, the less they value virtue," as if virtue and wealth were on the two pans of a scale, always tipping in opposite directions (550e). Finally, they pass a law making **property** the qualification for office.

Oligarchy has several great faults (551c–552e):

- It chooses rulers by **wealth**, not ability, as if one were to choose a ship's pilot by his property, and refuse to let a poor man steer, even if he were a better pilot.
- It is not one city but **two**, the rich and the poor, "living in the same place, always plotting against each other."
- It cannot easily fight wars, since it must either arm the poor, whom it fears more than the enemy, or fight with only a few.
- It allows a person to **sell** all his property and remain in the city as a member of no class, neither a merchant, nor a craftsman, nor a soldier, but a pauper. Such people are like **drones** in a hive, some stingless, who become beggars, and some with stings, who become criminals.

## The oligarchic man

The **oligarchic man** is the son of a timocratic man, who saw his father, a general or high official, suddenly ruined, put on trial, executed, exiled, or deprived of his property. Frightened and humbled by poverty, the son turns to **money-making**. He sets up the **appetitive**, money-loving part of his soul as "a great king within him," and makes reason and spirit its **slaves**, allowing reason to calculate only how to make more money, and spirit to admire only wealth and the wealthy (553a–d).

The oligarchic man is thrifty and hard-working, satisfying only his **necessary** desires, and restraining the others, not by persuasion or reason, but by **force** and fear, because he is afraid of losing his property. He is not at peace with himself, but "a kind of double man." He has a reputation for honesty, because he does not want to risk his money; but given the chance to cheat a helpless orphan, he would take it (554a–e).

## Democracy

Oligarchy, in turn, declines into **democracy**. The rulers of an oligarchy encourage the young to be **spendthrift**, since they make money by lending to them at interest and buying up their property. The ruined and indebted men, some of them nobles, remain in the city, "with stings and armed," hating those who have their property, and longing for revolution. The rich, meanwhile, become soft and luxurious, and the poor, lean and tanned, fighting beside them in battle, see that "these men are ours, for they are nothing" (556c–e). When the poor win, they kill some of their opponents, exile others, and give the rest an **equal share** in the constitution and offices, mostly assigned by **lot**. "That is how democracy comes into being" (557a).

Socrates's description of democracy is a brilliant satire of **Athens**, the city in which Plato lived, and which had executed Socrates:

> "First, they are free, and the city is full of freedom and free speech, and there is license in it to do whatever one wishes. … And where there is license, each person will arrange his own life privately as it pleases him. … So it may be the most beautiful of constitutions, like a many-colored cloak embroidered with every kind of flower. … And it is a convenient place to look for a constitution, since, because of its license, it contains every kind; it is like a **bazaar of constitutions**." (*Republic* 8.557b–d, plain rendering)

In a democracy, there is no compulsion to rule, even if one is able, or to be ruled, if one does not wish, or to go to war when others do, or to keep the peace when others do. Men condemned to death or exile stay in the city and walk around like heroes, unnoticed. Democracy is tolerant, scorning the careful education the ideal city required, and honoring anyone who says he is "well-disposed to the many." "It is a pleasant constitution, anarchic and many-colored, distributing a kind of **equality** to equals and unequals alike" (558a–c).

## The democratic man

The **democratic man** is the son of an oligarchic man, raised in his father's thrifty ways, but who tastes the honey of the drones, and falls in with fierce and clever companions who can provide every kind of pleasure. His **unnecessary** desires, which his father suppressed, now take over his soul, in which there is no knowledge or fine pursuits or true arguments to defend it. False and boastful arguments seize the "acropolis" of his soul, calling shame foolishness, moderation cowardice, and insolence good breeding, anarchy freedom, extravagance magnificence, and shamelessness courage (559d–561a).

Then, if he is lucky, and as he grows older, he lets some of the exiled desires return, and lives treating all his desires as **equal**:

> "He lives day by day, gratifying the desire that comes along: sometimes drinking heavily and listening to the flute, then drinking only water and dieting; now taking exercise, then idling and neglecting everything, and sometimes spending his time as if he were doing philosophy. Often he takes part in politics, leaping up and saying and doing whatever comes into his head. If he envies military men, he is carried in that direction; if money-makers, in that one. There is no order or necessity in his life, but he calls this life pleasant, free, and blessed, and follows it throughout." (*Republic* 8.561c–d, plain rendering)

He is "many-colored," like the democratic city, and "many men envy his life," because it contains the patterns of so many constitutions and characters.

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates describes democracy as "a bazaar of constitutions," full of freedom, but distributing "equality to equals and unequals alike." Is this a fair criticism of democracy, or a description of its virtues? Could the same features be both?`,
