      title: "The Republic and Its Readers",
      body: `## Has Socrates answered the challenge?

We began this course with Glaucon's question: **is it better to be just than unjust**, even for someone who could get away with injustice, and even if justice brought no rewards? Has Socrates answered it?

His answer, in summary, is this. Justice is the **right order** of the soul, in which reason rules, spirit supports reason, and appetite obeys. It is the **health** of the soul. The unjust person, whose soul is ruled by appetite or spirit, is like a city in civil war, or a tyranny, enslaved to the worst part of himself, full of fear, regret, and insatiable desire. The just person, ruled by reason that knows the Good, is free, at peace with himself, and enjoys the truest pleasures. So the just person is happier than the unjust, even in the worst circumstances. And, beyond this life, the soul is immortal, and the just will be rewarded.

Many readers have been persuaded. Others have raised serious objections, some of which you have met in this course:

- **The fallacy.** David Sachs argued that Socrates showed that **psychic** justice, the harmony of the soul, is good for its possessor, but not that it guarantees **ordinary** just behavior toward others, which was what Glaucon asked about.
- **The analogy.** Bernard Williams and others questioned whether the analogy between city and soul holds, and whether a just city would be made up of just individuals.
- **The philosophers.** The argument that the just life is happiest depends heavily on the claim that the philosopher, who knows the Good, has the best and most pleasant life. But few people can be philosophers in Plato's sense. Is the ordinary just person, who is not a philosopher, also happier than the successful villain? The Myth of Er suggests that virtue by habit, without philosophy, is unstable.
- **The return to the cave.** If the philosopher's life is best, it is not clear why it is just, or in his interest, to give it up to rule.

## The ideal city

The *Republic*'s political proposals have provoked even fiercer debate. **Karl Popper**, writing during the Second World War, attacked Plato as an enemy of the "**open society**," whose ideal city, with its rigid class structure, censorship, propaganda, eugenics, and rule by an unaccountable elite, was a blueprint for **totalitarianism**. Others have defended Plato, arguing that the city is a **pattern** for the soul rather than a practical political program, as Socrates himself suggests at the end of Book 9; that its purpose is to make visible what justice is, by imagining it on a large scale; and that its most radical features, the equality of women, the separation of power from wealth, the demand that rulers be wise and disinterested, are challenges to every society.

The American scholar **Allan Bloom**, whose translation you have used, argued in his interpretive essay, following his teacher **Leo Strauss**, that the *Republic* is deeply **ironic**: that its ideal city is deliberately shown to be impossible and even absurd, and that Plato's real teaching is that the perfect political order cannot be realized, and that philosophy and politics are always in tension. This reading too has been much disputed.

## The influence of the Republic

Whatever its merits, the *Republic* has shaped the Western tradition as few books have. Aristotle's *Politics* begins with a critique of it. The Stoic Zeno wrote a *Republic* of his own, and Cicero wrote a dialogue *On the Republic* in conscious imitation. **Augustine**'s *City of God*, which contrasts the earthly city with the heavenly, owes much to Plato's "pattern laid up in heaven." Thomas More's *Utopia* (1516), the founding work of a whole literary genre, was inspired by the *Republic*, as were the utopias and dystopias that followed it, from Francis Bacon's *New Atlantis* to Aldous Huxley's *Brave New World* (1932). The allegory of the cave has become a permanent part of the language of thought. The great English philosopher **Alfred North Whitehead** wrote, in 1929, that "the safest general characterization of the European philosophical tradition is that it consists of a series of footnotes to Plato."

## Reading the Republic again

The *Republic* is a book to be read more than once. On a first reading, one follows the argument and the images. On a second, one begins to see how the parts fit together: how the descent to the Piraeus anticipates the return to the cave; how the noble lie of Book 3 prepares for the myth of Er in Book 10; how Thrasymachus's tyrant, called happy and blessed in Book 1, becomes the most wretched of men in Book 9; how Cephalus's fear of punishment in the afterlife is answered by Er's vision; how Glaucon, the lover of luxury who wanted relishes for the city of pigs, is led, step by step, to the vision of the Good. The book itself is an education, a turning of the soul.

## The question remains

Plato's question has not been settled. Every generation asks it again: Is it better to be just, even when no one is watching? Does morality serve our interests, or only those of others? What kind of person, and what kind of community, should we try to be? The *Republic* does not end the conversation. But it shows, more powerfully than any other book, why the conversation matters, and that "the question is not a small one, but how one should live" (352d).

## The Republic and the Bible

Christian readers have long noticed both the kinship and the distance between Plato's vision and their own. Both teach that the soul matters more than the body, that wickedness enslaves, and that there is a true city beyond the cities of this world. But where Plato's soul climbs toward the Good by its own effort, through mathematics and dialectic, the Christian tradition speaks of a Good that descends: "For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son" (John 3:16, King James Version). Augustine, who owed much to the Platonists, wrote in his *Confessions* (7.9) that in their books he had found the eternal Word, but not the Word made flesh. The comparison has been one of the great conversations of Western thought.

> **Pause and consider.** At the beginning of this course, you were asked to write down whether you think it is always in a person's interest to be just. Look at your answer again. Has the *Republic* changed your view? Why or why not?`,
